  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A line 丨 through the center of a box 口"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "A line passing through the middle of a box, representing the meaning \"middle\"."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "内也。从口。丨，上下通。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形) – a flagpole, as depicted in its original form 𠁩. Based on archaeological evidence, the middle box has been interpreted as a drum (建鼓). This flagpole with a drum was placed in the center of a field to gather people and to detect the direction of the wind. In addition, the pronunciation of 中 (OC *tuŋ, *tuŋs) is reminiscent of the beating of a drum.\nShuowen interprets the character as a vertical stroke 丨 passing through the center of 口, indicating the center.\nIt has also been interpreted as an arrow in the center of a target.\n“Middle; centre” (Pronunciation 1) > “to hit the centre; to attain” (Pronunciation 2).\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *t/duŋ. Cognate with Tibetan གཞུང (gzhung, “middle, center”).\nRelated to:\n* 衷 (OC *tuŋ, *tuŋs, “middle; inner garment; inner feelings”);\n* 仲 (OC *duŋs, “second (of the brothers or months)”)."
    }
  ],
